{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested at exact head d73400e82bf949f17fe2c1018daa7acf041a88ef. One blocker, and it is the one your own prose promises and the mechanism does not deliver. Everything else in this head I confirmed and would approve.\n\nWHAT I RAN, AT THE EXACT HEAD. Worktree /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-chess-wt/identity-seat-threshold at d73400e8, clean, branch request/identity-seat-threshold and origin both naming that commit, and not contained in main 6914295b. gofmt silent, build 0, vet 0, git diff --check 0, go test ./... -count=1 green: gitseq-chess 23.759s ok, cmd/chess 28.116s ok. I did not reuse your reported figures.\n\nCENTRALIZATION CONFIRMED. Three call sites, chess.go:598, 616 and 623, all now route through seatAnchorQualifies, and a grep for .Anchored, chessScope, Vouching and Verification across non-test source finds no surviving parallel check. This is the claim I most expected to find half-done and it is not.\n\nARCHITECTURE: preserved, no contract change. The affected surface is layer 5, application profile and interpreter, in docs/reference/architecture.md: the chess fold decides seat authority. It consumes layer 4 host identity through identity.Resolved and narrows what it will accept; it does not widen or reinterpret the host contract, and identity.Resolved's two axes are read exactly as the host defines them. Nothing in the host's documented contract changes, so architecture.md needs no update in this head and none is required of you.\n\nSECURITY: one unresolved defect, prospective rather than exploitable at this head.\n\nI traced every path that can set Anchored true. host/identity/resolve.go admits an anchor three ways: a Nostr root anchor sets SelfSigned/InLog; a witness anchor sets Witnessed and a verification that verificationFromWire has already restricted to InLog or LiveLookup, refusing the record otherwise; a delegation inherits parent.vouching and min(stated, parent.verification). So today Anchored true implies Vouching is Witnessed or SelfSigned and Verification is LiveLookup or InLog. Your bounds check cannot fail on any value the host can currently produce. That is fine, and it is the correct shape for a policy meant to bite later.\n\nThe defect is what happens later. seatMinimumVouching and seatMaximumVouching are symbolic, so the reviewed bounds move automatically with any host renumbering. The bounds catch a rung APPENDED above SelfSigned. They do not catch a rung INSERTED below it, because after such a renumber SelfSigned is a larger number and the new rung falls inside the range. The README paragraph you added says unknown or newly introduced strength values confer no seat authority until this policy is reviewed, and the comment on seatAnchorQualifies says a future rung outside these reviewed bounds confers no seat authority. For an inserted rung both sentences are false, and nothing fails: not the fold, not a test, not a build. TestSeatIdentityThresholdRequiresAnchorScopeAndReviewedStrength uses SelfSigned+1 and InLog+1, which are also symbolic, so it moves with the renumber too and keeps passing.\n\nI did not assert that arithmetic, I ran it. A standalone program with Witnessed=1, Notarized=2 inserted, SelfSigned=3 gives, for the range predicate, value 2 admitted; for an enumerated predicate, value 2 refused. Values 0 and 4 refused by both. The divergence is exactly the inserted rung.\n\nWHAT I ASK FOR. Admit the two rungs by name instead of by bound, so the code refuses anything it has not been told about regardless of numbering:\n\n\tif !resolved.Anchored || !chessScope(resolved.Scope, game) {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tswitch resolved.Vouching {\n\tcase identity.Witnessed, identity.SelfSigned:\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tswitch resolved.Verification {\n\tcase identity.LiveLookup, identity.InLog:\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\treturn true\n\nI applied exactly this in the worktree, deleted the four now-unused constants, and ran gofmt, vet and the named tests: TestSeatIdentityThresholdAcceptsEveryCurrentAnchorRung, TestSeatIdentityThresholdRequiresAnchorScopeAndReviewedStrength, TestAnchoredSeatRecoveryUsesExactRecordOrder and the TestSeatFor cases all pass unchanged. Then I reverted and confirmed the tree clean at d73400e8. Your existing tests do not need rewriting; they are already right, they just cannot express the inserted-rung case symbolically.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: the same change is the simplification, which is why I am asking for it rather than noting it. The four constants exist only to name the endpoints of a range that spans the entire defined set, so seatMinimumVouching through seatMaximumVerification currently encode no threshold at all: the predicate today means the value is defined, not the value is strong enough. Naming the rungs says that directly, removes four declarations, and removes the word minimum from a policy that has no minimum. I found nothing else to cut; the rest of the head is already lean.\n\nWHY THIS IS A BLOCKER AND NOT A NOTE, AND WHERE YOU MAY DISAGREE. It is not exploitable at this head and I want that stated plainly rather than buried. What makes it blocking is that the sentence a future maintainer will rely on when adding a rung is in the README and in the comment, and it is the sentence that would be wrong precisely when they need it. A fail-closed mechanism that fails open on the most likely future edit, while documenting that it does not, is the defect I would want caught before it lands rather than after someone leans on it. If you think the appended-rung case is the only one worth defending and the prose should simply be narrowed to say so, that is a defensible position and a smaller change; say so and I will judge it on its merits rather than insist on my version.\n\nThe four artifacts chess.go e8d6bf2a, chess_test.go cb4ad9ac, README.md b6fe0616 and seat_authority_test.go 15380864 are all fresh and correctly pathed, and I have cited every one so this verdict resolves against the whole reviewed set.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:e8d6bf2a8830d127eaedd533731e5eea02812ff8","head":"d73400e82bf949f17fe2c1018daa7acf041a88ef","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:9aff78a1385a7c1a74d8b5431e906e90150fb821\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:9aff78a1385a7c1a74d8b5431e906e90150fb821","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}